notes=r'''Manuscript page 12 has a curved enclosure at the upper right; the lower-left number is 14. The page proposes a free basis for Φ_0, replaces an older two-degree notation by φ_i^{β,α}, gives a multiplication rule in the left margin, and begins a Proposition continued on scan 016. The upper half contains two cancelled definitions and two cancelled range/basis blocks, all retained. The marginal instruction says the elements and multiplication “devraient|devront être donnés au no 6”; its verbal ending remains doubtful. Another margin says Z can be replaced by any ring. The lower paragraph says the multiplication is independent of the choices, and the Proposition asks for the equivalence of the Lefschetz condition with a graded-ring homomorphism.

**N16-015-C1 — the written basis bound.** The live bound is visibly 0≤α,β≤2n−i, in both the ink and the two drafts. At n=1, take the ordered exterior basis (1,e,f,v=e∧f). Exterior multiplication is L=E_(v,1), contraction is C=E_(1,v), and the degree projectors are P_0=CL, P_2=LC, P_1=I−P_0−P_2. The written range includes i=0, α=0, β=2. Its element is φ_0^{2,0}=L²P_0=0. A family containing this zero vector is linearly dependent, so the stated basis assertion with that bound fails. The source bound remains unchanged.

**N16-015-R1 — exact finite refinement and multiplication certificate.** In this n=1 example the five matrices P_0,P_2,P_1,L,C form a Z-basis of the generated algebra: closure follows from their 25 products, and independence follows by inspecting the entries (1,1),(v,v),(e,e),(v,1),(1,v). More explicitly the ring morphism Mat_2(Z)⊕Z→End_Z(Λ Z²) acts by the 2×2 block on span(1,v) and by the scalar on span(e,f). Its kernel is zero because each block is visible, and its image has exactly the five listed matrix generators. The bounds 0≤α,β≤n−i give exactly those five matrices at n=1. For these matrices, φ_(i′)^{β′,α′}φ_i^{β,α} is zero for i′≠i or α′≠β and equals φ_i^{β′,α} otherwise. The explicit 25 products are checked in scripts/basis_check_015.py. This is a calculation for the specified rank-two exterior module, without a general integral basis theorem.

**N16-015-C2 — multiplication with the contraction from scan 014.** Reuse the explicit n=2 exterior module from N16-014-C2. On span_Q(1,ξ,ξ²), L raises one step, the chain lowering B lowers one step, and the raw contraction C satisfies Cξ=2 and Cξ²=2ξ. Let P project onto 1 and Q onto ξ along the other two vectors. The proposed elements CP_(2,1)=CQ and LP are C Q and L P on this chain. Their product CQLP sends 1 to 2 and annihilates the other basis vectors, so it is 2P. The marginal rule would give P. **N16-015-R2** records the explicit relation C=2B on this chain and hence CQLP=2P; BQLP=P. This uses the same concrete operators and the exact comparison already checked on scan 014, and preserves the source formulas unchanged.

The multiplication margin has one equality, surviving at the right of its cancelled old left member, and a straight vertical separator. C duplicated that equality in a separate cancelled product. C also duplicated the crossed β−α exponent of the lower old L_0 as a separate cancelled expression after the live condition above it. Both placements are corrected from the crops. The parenthesis reads “ni du fait qu'on est sur l'anneau Z ....”, and the abbreviated phrase reads “élts de cette base”. The adjective graduée remains feminine after the replacement of algèbre by anneau. The old opening, several cancelled indices and degree constraints, and the E-like cancellation before End remain doubtful; the mathematics has not been used to fill them. The Proposition is continued on the following scan, so its complete claim is assessed there.'''
